{
  "dom_id": "step-q115542",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-3x}.$ Now consider the following properties of $f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$\n\n- The point $(-11,-24)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{b-3x}\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{b}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ which is equivalent to $x\\le 1.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\n- Since the point $(-11,-24)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-11) & =a\\sqrt{3-3\\cdot (-11)} \\\\ -24 & =6a \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=-4\\sqrt{3-3x}$ and\n\n$$\na+b=-4+3=-1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
